# FEATURE-LEGACY-PATIENT-DOCUMENT-COMPLETENESS-1 — Kelengkapan Arsip Pasien Legacy

A read-only monitor over the legacy archive. It reports which **legacy-imported**
patients do not yet hold both a published legacy RME and a published legacy
odontogram. It decides nothing and mutates nothing.

Reads with, never against: rule 90 (legacy RME archive), rule 168 (legacy patient
staged import), rule 170 (single active document per patient), rule 171 (batch
review/publish), and the Front Office branch rules.

---

## LPC-R1 — Provenance is `mst_patients.import_batch_id`, and nothing else

A patient belongs on this page **only** when `import_batch_id IS NOT NULL` —
the canonical marker written by `LegacyPatientImportService::commit()`.

**Never** infer legacy provenance from `created_at`, an old-looking Nomor RM
year, the medical-record-number format, the branch, a missing NIK/KTP, the
absence of a native RME, the presence of a legacy document, or an operator's
guess. Every one of those lists natively registered patients.

**`Patient::isLegacyWithoutBranch()` MEANS SOMETHING ELSE** — `branch_id IS NULL`
(Sprint 23.10). It is not a provenance predicate. Using it here lists native
branchless patients and misses every imported patient that has a branch.

The predicate lives in the repository's single base query, so no filter,
parameter or crafted request can switch it off. Native exclusion is structural,
not a property of the default filter.

## LPC-R2 — Native patients never appear, under any filter

Including the `complete` audit filter. If a native patient ever shows up, the
provenance predicate has been bypassed — that is a defect, not a filter choice.

## LPC-R3 — Per-document state is derived, in the occupancy order

```
published record        -> PUBLISHED   (the only complete state)
else slot-occupying row -> IN_PROGRESS
else void record        -> VOID
else                    -> MISSING
```

The archive is consulted **before** the staging table, matching
`LegacySingleActiveDocumentService::occupancyFor()`. Reverse that order and a
void-then-republished record reads as in-progress forever, because a staging row
keeps its historical `PUBLISHED` status permanently.

Assert the agreement against the **real** service, never against a copy of its
rules — a copy passes while production drifts.

## LPC-R4 — IN_PROGRESS is not MISSING, and the distinction is load-bearing

A slot-occupying staging row still owns the patient's slot, so a new upload
would be refused by the single-active-document guard. Reporting it as
"Belum Ada — upload" sends an operator to an action the server rejects.

`FAILED` is **IN_PROGRESS**: `FAILED -> QUEUED` is a legal transition, so the
import is retryable and still holds the slot. `CANCELLED` is terminal and
produced no archive, so it is MISSING.

## LPC-R5 — A soft-deleted staging row still occupies the slot

The staging aggregates and the representative-status lookups apply **no**
`deleted_at` filter, matching `firstSlotOccupyingForPatient()`'s `withTrashed()`.
A soft delete never releases a document slot; only an audited CANCEL or VOID
does. Filtering trashed rows out would show "Belum Ada" for a slot the server
still refuses to re-fill.

Go through `DB::table()` rather than the Eloquent models so there is no global
soft-delete scope to forget to disable.

## LPC-R6 — The two slots are independent

A published RME never substitutes for a missing odontogram, or the reverse.
There is deliberately **no** patient-wide "has any legacy document" predicate
anywhere — that shortcut is how the two slots get fused.

Completeness is per PATIENT; state is per DOCUMENT. They are different
questions: a patient with a published RME and no odontogram has a PUBLISHED
document state and an INCOMPLETE verdict.

## LPC-R7 — A soft-deleted patient is not a migration target

Excluded in the base query, not by a filter, so no caller can ask for one.
An **inactive** patient is still listed: "inactive" is an operational flag about
the patient, not a statement that their historical archive no longer needs
filing.

## LPC-R8 — "Needs an upload" is MISSING **or** VOID

Both leave the slot free, so both are the same next action. The filter keys on
availability; the STATUS COLUMN is what tells the operator that one is a
correction and the other a first filing. Filter on availability, display the
reason — never merge the two.

## LPC-R9 — The page is read-only, structurally

The repository interface exposes no `create`, `update`, `delete`, `upsert`,
`insert`, `transition`, `publish`, `void` or `cancel`, and must never gain one.
The module registers exactly **one** route and it is a `GET`. The page creates
no patient, import, archive record, clinic visit, medical record, odontogram,
invoice, payment, lab order or SATUSEHAT candidate; it reviews, publishes, voids
and cancels nothing; it assigns no wave and changes no branch.

## LPC-R10 — Authorization is a dedicated read permission

`view_legacy_patient_archive_completeness`. Granted to **Supervisor RME**,
**Admin Klinik** and **Front Office** (Super Admin via the single global
`Gate::before`). Doctor, Kasir, Perawat, Owner, Admin Lab, Technician, Admin
Warehouse, Finance and Kepala Cabang are denied.

Never reuse an intake permission (`view_legacy_*_imports`) for this page: every
branch upload operator holds one, so reuse would decide the audience as a side
effect of who may upload.

The permission must stay **absent** from every `GOVERNANCE_PERMISSIONS` list, so
granting it can never widen a holder's branch scope.

## LPC-R11 — Front Office IS the clinic admin identity in this system

`Front Office` is defined as the exact union of the two legacy roles it merged
(`FrontOfficeRole::LEGACY_ADMIN_CLINIC` ∪ `LEGACY_KASIR`), and
`FrontOfficeRoleTest` derives that union from their arrays **specifically so a
permission added to Admin Klinik cannot silently skip the merged role**.
Production holds **zero** Admin Klinik accounts.

So a permission intended for the clinic admin must be granted to **both** roles.
Granting Admin Klinik alone breaks a tested invariant and reaches nobody.

The measured consequence is stated, never hidden: four generic front-desk
accounts share that role and gain the page too. They already hold
`manage patients`, `view_rme_patient_reports` and both legacy intake view
permissions, so no new patient field is disclosed, and they stay branch-pinned.

## LPC-R12 — Branch scope is borrowed, resolved server-side, and fails closed

Governance tier = `LegacyImportHubService::GOVERNANCE_PERMISSIONS`, **referenced
rather than copied**: one definition shared with the sibling hub page. Everyone
else resolves to their own `BranchContext` branch, and only when it is an active
RME branch. Unresolvable, MAIN, or a non-RME branch all yield an **empty** list,
which the repository turns into an impossible predicate — never "no filter".

**Do not reuse `LegacyOdontogramWorkspaceScope`**: its governance set includes
`create_legacy_odontogram_imports`, which Admin Klinik and Front Office hold, so
it would promote every branch intake operator to estate-wide visibility.

**Do not reuse `RmeWorkingBranchScope`**: that is the clinic-operations authority
and fails closed to empty without an active online context. The legacy archive's
own authority is the BranchContext-based workspace scope.

## LPC-R13 — A requested branch can only narrow

The authorized set is resolved first, then intersected with any requested branch.
A branch the actor may not read is **dropped**, so a crafted `?branch_id=` leaves
the scope unchanged. The FormRequest's `exists` rule is a usability check, not a
security one. A branch filter never widens a scope and never leaks another branch
through any status filter, search term or page.

All counters respect the actor's scope. A branch-pinned actor never sees an
estate-wide number, and the branch filter only ever offers branches inside their
scope.

## LPC-R14 — A provenance-less legacy patient is governance-only

`branch_id IN (…)` is never true for `NULL`, so a branchless legacy patient is
invisible to a pinned actor for free. The governance tier sees it, matching how
`LegacyRmeWorkspaceScope` treats provenance-less archive rows.

## LPC-R15 — PII boundary

The row object carries the Nomor RM, the patient name, the branch, the two
document states, the verdict, the archive dates and the batch id. **Nothing
else.** No KTP/NIK, no address, no phone or WhatsApp number, no e-mail, no date
of birth, no occupation, no clinical content, no document title or description,
no storage path and no checksum.

The property set is pinned by a test, so a new field cannot reach the template
unreviewed. The row DTO is the disclosure boundary: a Blade template must not be
able to reach a column the report did not intend to publish.

## LPC-R16 — No invented statuses

Every label maps onto a value the backend actually holds. A label with no state
behind it makes an operator wait for a transition that can never happen.

## LPC-R17 — Set-based, constant query count

Four grouped aggregates LEFT JOINed, bounded by the number of DOCUMENTS rather
than patients. Correlated EXISTS is rejected: the filter runs over the whole
scope before pagination, so its cost would grow with the patient count.

The property to assert is **constancy**, not a ceiling — a per-row lookup added
to a 3-row fixture fits inside almost any headroom. Measure the same page at two
very different sizes.

Pagination is mandatory and the order is deterministic (Nomor RM, then id), so no
row repeats or is skipped between pages.

## LPC-R18 — Portable SQL, verified on PostgreSQL

`max(case when … then 1 else 0 end)` rather than `bool_or`;
`max(case when … then date end)` rather than `FILTER (WHERE …)`;
never `whereJsonLength` on a `jsonb` object. A report whose query only works on
one engine is a report nobody can test. Verify on PostgreSQL 16, production's
major version — a green SQLite run cannot prove it.

## LPC-R19 — LIKE escaping uses `!`, never a backslash

A backslash inside the single-quoted `ESCAPE` literal makes PDO's placeholder
rewriter treat the closing quote as escaped on `pdo_pgsql` through PHP 8.3; the
literal runs on, swallows the following placeholders, and every search returns
`SQLSTATE[HY093] Invalid parameter number`. That defect took patient search down
in production once already.

## LPC-R20 — Add an index only when a plan proves one is needed

Measured on the real production estate (1519 legacy patients): 0.552 ms
execution, 14 shared buffer hits, an Index Scan on
`mst_patients_medical_record_number_unique` feeding the ORDER BY so `LIMIT 25`
reads 27 rows — no `Seq Scan on mst_patients`. **No index was added.**

That plan is measured at today's document volume (19 RME + 1 odontogram
records). The patient-side scaling is index-driven; the document-side aggregates
would move to index-based group aggregates using the existing
`(patient_id, status)` indexes as volume grows.

## LPC-R21 — Row actions, if ever added, create no bypass

A link to an existing canonical flow is permitted only when the actor already
holds that permission, the state is eligible, wave/admission applies and the
single-active guard allows it. This sprint shipped **no** row action: a purely
read-only page was the explicitly acceptable scope, and it removes any chance of
pointing an operator at a refused upload.

## LPC-R22 — The sidebar is a convenience, never the boundary

The entry is gated by the same read permission the route is, and the group's
`@canany` admits that permission so a read-only grantee is not left with an
invisible group. It is deliberately **not** wrapped in a `migrationEnabled()`
check like its siblings: those guard intake, whose resting state is OFF, and a
backlog monitor that vanished whenever intake was closed would be unavailable
exactly when an operator wants to plan the next wave.

Reading what has already been published is a different concern from being
allowed to publish more.

The route name shares no prefix with any sibling, so exactly one entry lights up.
`settings.legacy-imports.*` is the hub's matcher — a child name under it would
highlight two entries at once.

## LPC-R23 — Assert the controller's re-check with the middleware lifted

Every denial test is answered by the route's `permission:` middleware, which runs
first, so none of them can see the controller's own `abort_unless` disappear.
Lift the middleware to make the second layer observable. A route file is edited
far more often than a controller, and a middleware list that silently loses an
entry must not silently open a page over patient data.

## LPC-R24 — One scope definition, consumed and never restated

`scopedPatients()` is the only place the soft-delete exclusion, the provenance
predicate and the branch scope are expressed. The listing, the counters, the
branch filter **and** the per-page lookups all build on it.

A second, hand-restated copy is the defect even while it agrees. The branch
filter originally restated the three predicates and had **already** drifted: it
applied a bare `whereIn` with no unscoped arm, so a governance actor saw a
branchless legacy patient in the rows with no corresponding branch option — two
definitions of "a legacy patient in my scope" on one page. The direction was
narrower, so nothing leaked; what it cost was the guarantee, because the next
edit to the provenance predicate would have reached one surface and silently
missed the other.

Row-set assertions cannot see this. The predicates are therefore counted at the
source — each exactly once, with `applyBranchScope()` called from exactly one
place — so a restatement fails loudly instead of agreeing quietly.

## LPC-R25 — The per-page lookups authorise; they do not trust their caller

`slotOccupyingRmeStatuses()`, `slotOccupyingOdontogramStatuses()` and
`reviewBlockedPatientIds()` receive explicit patient ids. **The ids narrow to a
page; the query authorises.** Both the branch scope and the provenance
predicate are re-applied inside each one, as a sub-select over the same
`scopedPatients()` definition.

Today the only caller derives those ids from the already-scoped paginator, so
this narrows nothing — which is exactly why its absence was invisible, and
exactly why it is cheap to add. What it buys: a future row action, drilldown or
AJAX refresh that takes patient ids from a request cannot read another branch's
legacy staging status or reviewer holds, and the interface's own promise
("every query here is unconditionally restricted") is structurally true rather
than a property of who happens to call it.

An interface docblock asserting a control the code does not have is worse than
no docblock: it tells the next author the check already exists.
